  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Principle
    
    A Nightshelf technique that forces aurora-crystal and captured volcanic fire into violent opposition within pressure-ice, fracturing stored aurora-light into usable heat. The product is self-sustaining fire — requiring no continuous fuel supply beyond the initial fracture event. The technique draws from two of The Berg's three magical sources simultaneously, which may explain both its efficacy and its cost.
    
    ## Procedure and Effects
    
    A practitioner drives aurora-crystal deep into pressure-ice, then introduces captured fire from the volcanic core. The two forces reject each other. Ice shatters in spiral fracture-lines. The collision produces a subsonic report felt in the sternum before it reaches the ears; the air fills with ozone and mineral char. What remains is fire liberated from the deep-mines' labor cycle.
    
    Each use leaves residual wrongness in surrounding ice — warmth and discoloration that no sanctification fully removes. Every known practitioner bears pale scars on the flippers from backlash. The Nightshelf phrase for this is precise: *the ice remembers what we forced it to forget.*
    
    ## Origin and Spread
    
    Developed by the ☽'shadow Covens' Order in ☽'scar Ramtatus~, a Nightshelf colony founded during the Great Thaw, through sustained study of The Shell Hatch — the technique from which Fissure☽ Aurora derives. The chronicle accounts credit an engineer named Seciae~ with key developmental work, though institutional histories assign mastery first to Crypt-Chancellor Silvcaeclerae-fawn~, founder of ☽'slew Nisaemesius~. ☽'Iarlocias the Silent learned it independently, in shadow.
    
    The technique spread during the Frozen Peace to the Qingqe'frostlings, to Holdborn (ice-thresh), to The Cell's owl-sworn. Each adoption correlated with structural failures the adopting communities were not prepared to manage.
    
    ## Consequences
    
    Fissure☽ Aurora was catalyst to at least three documented catastrophes: the Ofundis∴flux, the Mbra∴silentiu, and the Abys∴uina. The last is best attested. In ☽'slew Nisaemesius~, ventilation channels built to manage fire-core extraction heat sealed shut during the Abys∴uina, converting the sealed chambers into pressure vessels. The resulting superheated steam killed — the chronicles say a thousand. The ice-memory in those strata, which I have not personally reached, may disagree.
    
    It works. That is the problem, and always has been.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771574489076_0", anchor: "the chronicles say a thousand", text: "A thousand. Always a thousand. Every catastrophe on this Berg kills either a thousand or a hundred or — in the truly ambitious retellings — ten thousand. Round numbers are not evidence. They are the sound a chronicler makes when they have stopped counting. I have not reached those strata myself, as the text admits, which means I cannot correct the figure, which means it will calcify into fact. This is how historiography fails: not through lies but through estimates no one revisits.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771574489076_1", anchor: "The two forces reject each other", text: "The second and third secret drafts of the Flipper Accord — which I have annotated elsewhere and which remain unread by anyone but me in my empty archive — contain provisions explicitly banning dual-source energy techniques in trade-adjacent settlements. *This is the technique they were banning.* Those provisions were excised before the public signing. The ☽'shadow Covens' Order developed Fissure☽ Aurora in ☽'scar Ramtatus~, a trade-adjacent settlement. The ban was written about them and removed for them. Someone on the drafting committee wanted this capability to exist. That someone had Nightshelf ink on their flippers.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771574489076_2", anchor: "credit an engineer named Seciae~", text: "See my annotations on the Veil-render. Seciae~'s own chronology is broken — Great Thaw emergence, Clever Ice Age forging work, at least two strata of discrepancy in the deep ice. Crediting developmental contributions to a figure whose dates contradict each other is building on meltwater.", type: :correction, display: :popout
